WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.289
Take a made-up product.

2
00:00:04.289 --> 00:00:06.454
Eight services, three Pods each.

3
00:00:06.454 --> 00:00:09.025
That is twenty four Pods on two nodes.

4
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.268
Every mesh adds proxies to the path of each call.

5
00:00:12.268 --> 00:00:16.339
The real question is where those proxies run, and what they cost.

6
00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:19.227
Linkerd puts a sidecar in every Pod.

7
00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:23.642
Its proxy is linkerd2-proxy, a micro-proxy written in Rust.

8
00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:25.663
Linkerd does not use Envoy.

9
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:32.420
A call from one Pod to another leaves through its own proxy, and arrives through the other Pod's proxy.

10
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.091
Two proxies on every call.

11
00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:46.071
Before you write a single policy, you get mutual TLS for all TCP traffic between meshed Pods, golden metrics for every workload, and load balancing per request.

12
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:48.527
Cilium works the other way.

13
00:00:48.527 --> 00:00:52.729
It is already the network plugin, so it sits on every packet.

14
00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:53.556
No sidecars.

15
00:00:53.733 --> 00:01:01.902
Pod networking, Service load balancing and network policy run in the Linux kernel, as small eBPF programs.

16
00:01:01.902 --> 00:01:03.752
No trip through a proxy.

17
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:06.864
Anything that needs HTTP goes to Envoy.

18
00:01:06.864 --> 00:01:11.072
But only one Envoy per node, the cilium-envoy DaemonSet.

19
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:15.132
Between nodes, Cilium can encrypt with WireGuard or IPsec.

20
00:01:15.132 --> 00:01:16.665
That protects the wire.

21
00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:21.463
But it proves which node a packet came from, not which workload sent it.

22
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.432
Mutual TLS between workload identities is where Cilium is still moving.

23
00:01:26.432 --> 00:01:28.542
Its replacement design is Beta.

24
00:01:28.542 --> 00:01:33.033
So if that is your main reason for a mesh, check its status first.

25
00:01:33.733 --> 00:01:34.670
Now the bill.

26
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.784
Go back to one sidecar per Pod, and measure one proxy, as the lab does.

27
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:45.652
In the worked example, one Envoy hop added about 0.6 ms to the median call.

28
00:01:45.652 --> 00:01:49.312
A sidecar mesh puts two such hops on every call.

29
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:52.948
An idle Envoy held about 64 MB of memory.

30
00:01:52.948 --> 00:01:55.240
You pay that for every Pod.

31
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.566
Twenty four times sixty four.

32
00:01:57.566 --> 00:02:02.197
About 1.5 gigabytes of proxies, before they carry any traffic.

33
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:04.526
And the bill has more lines.

34
00:02:04.526 --> 00:02:06.063
Upgrades of every proxy.

35
00:02:06.063 --> 00:02:09.265
Debugging, because a call has more places to fail.

36
00:02:09.265 --> 00:02:13.363
And people, who own the certificates and read the release notes.

37
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:18.504
For a handful of services owned by one team, I would not start with a mesh.

38
00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:21.307
Tools you already know cover most of the list.

39
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:24.561
NetworkPolicy decides who may talk to whom.

40
00:02:24.561 --> 00:02:28.159
A shared client library sets timeouts and retries.

41
00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:30.749
One Gateway handles TLS at the edge.

42
00:02:30.749 --> 00:02:35.787
And OpenTelemetry and Prometheus give you per call metrics and traces.

43
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:43.641
For our made-up product, with one team and no auditor asking for mutual TLS, the checklist says: no mesh yet.

44
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:53.764
The answer changes as services and teams multiply, with many languages, or when an auditor asks for encryption and identity on every call.

45
00:02:53.933 --> 00:02:56.032
Then go one namespace at a time.

46
00:02:56.032 --> 00:02:59.312
First, mesh it, and only watch the golden metrics.

47
00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.804
You will find calls nobody knew about.

48
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.509
Next, mutual TLS in permissive mode, so meshed callers encrypt and everyone else still works.

49
00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:11.379
Then strict, once every caller is meshed.

50
00:03:11.533 --> 00:03:15.463
Then authorization policies, starting with sensitive data.

51
00:03:15.463 --> 00:03:19.461
And traffic policies, retries, timeouts and canaries, last.

52
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.484
Decide first.

53
00:03:20.484 --> 00:03:24.563
And if a mesh is worth it, switch it on one layer at a time.
